resolved.description:
[s0l0!-header][h1]Event Listeners[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

The [b]addEventListener[/b]() method attaches an event handler to an element without overwriting existing event handlers. You can add [i]many [/i]event handlers to one element.
You can also add many event handlers of the same type to one element, i.e., two "click" events.[code format="js" ]element.[b]addEventListener[/b](event, function, useCapture);[/code]
The first parameter is the event's [b]type [/b](like "click" or "mousedown").
The second parameter is the [b]function [/b]we want to call when the event occurs.
The third parameter is a Boolean value specifying whether to use event [b]bubbling [/b]or event [b]capturing[/b]. This parameter is optional, and will be described in the next lesson.
[note]Note that you don't use the "[b]on[/b]" prefix for this event; use "[b]click[/b]" instead of "[b]onclick[/b]".[/note]
[s0l0!-header][b]Example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="js" ]element.[b]addEventListener("click", myFunction)[/b];
element.[b]addEventListener("mouseover", myFunction);[/b]

function myFunction() {
  alert("Hello World!");
}[/code]
This adds two event listeners to the element.
We can remove one of the listeners: [code format="js" ]element.[b]removeEventListener[/b]("mouseover", myFunction);[/code]
Let's create an event handler that removes itself after being executed: [code format="html" codeId="965"]<button id="demo">Start</button>

<script>
var btn = document.getElementById("demo");
[b]btn.addEventListener[/b]("click", myFunction);

function myFunction() {
  alert(Math.random());
  btn.[b]removeEventListener[/b]("click", myFunction);
}
</script>[/code]
After clicking the button, an alert with a random number displays and the event listener is removed.
[note]Internet Explorer version 8 and lower do not support the [b]addEventListener[/b]() and [b]removeEventListener[/b]() methods. However, you can use the document.[b]attachEvent[/b]() method to attach event handlers in Internet Explorer.[/note]

